WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.672
In the last years of Western Zhou, King You had a consort named Baosi.

00:00:04.022 --> 00:00:07.670
Baosi rarely smiled, and King You longed to make her happy.

00:00:08.020 --> 00:00:10.708
One day, King You came up with a bad idea.

00:00:11.058 --> 00:00:14.226
He ordered the beacon fires — the signal that warns of an enemy attack — to be lit.

00:00:14.576 --> 00:00:17.528
The beacons were a signal for help, not to be lit carelessly.

00:00:17.878 --> 00:00:21.166
Seeing the beacons, the lords hurriedly led their troops to the rescue.

00:00:21.516 --> 00:00:24.756
They found there was no enemy at all — but Baosi laughed.

00:00:25.106 --> 00:00:27.938
The lords led their troops home in anger.

00:00:28.288 --> 00:00:32.416
To make Baosi laugh again, King You lit the beacons again and again.

00:00:32.766 --> 00:00:35.838
Slowly the lords realized they were being fooled, time after time.

00:00:36.188 --> 00:00:38.708
They thought: next time, we will not believe so easily.

00:00:39.058 --> 00:00:41.890
Later, a real enemy truly came.

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:45.768
The beacons were lit, but no lord arrived in time.

00:00:46.118 --> 00:00:49.910
And so the Western Zhou capital lost its protection.

00:00:50.260 --> 00:00:53.212
This story tells us that trust is precious.

00:00:53.562 --> 00:00:57.210
Once trust is lost, lighting the beacons again is of no use.

00:00:57.560 --> 00:01:01.520
This lesson has been remembered for more than two thousand years.

